<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minox is a subminiature camera designed by the Riga-born engineer Walter Zapp and first manufactured in 1938 by the state electrical works VEF in Riga. Small enough to fit in the palm and using film 9.5 millimetres wide, it became famous as a spy camera used by intelligence services during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Production later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where the Minox company continued after 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minox.jpg" alt="Minox" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gisling · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tele_Minox.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Walter Zapp, a Baltic German born in Riga in 1905, conceived the camera in Reval, now Tallinn, where he had worked as an engraver and for a photographer. The idea of a camera that could always be carried grew out of talks with his friends Nikolai Nylander, who coined the name Minox and drew its mouse logo, and Richard Jürgens, who funded the project. Unable to find backing for production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, Jürgens approached the electrotechnical works VEF in Riga, where Zapp demonstrated his prototype with enlargements from its negatives. VEF secured patents on his inventions in at least 18 countries and kept producing the camera through the Soviet and German occupations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> until 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Riga Minox had a brass chassis in a stainless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> shell that slid open to uncover the lens and viewfinder and closed to advance the film. It measured 80 by 27 by 16 mm and weighed 130 g. Its 15 mm f/3.5 Minostigmat lens focused as close as 20 cm and gave such depth of field that no diaphragm was needed, and a metal foil curtain shutter sat behind a protective window. Each frame measured 8 by 11 mm on unperforated film held in a twin-chamber cartridge of up to 50 exposures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Zapp meant it as a camera for everyone, but its cost made it a luxury item, and its size and close focusing drew intelligence agencies in America, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the Soviet bloc. It was used by both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> and Allied agents; one record shows the US Office of Strategic Services buying 25 in 1942, and the Soviet spy John A. Walker Jr. photographed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> ciphers with a Minox C. A measuring chain supplied with most cameras made copying letter-sized documents easy.</p><p>After the war Minox GmbH began making the aluminium-shelled Minox II in 1948 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giessen">Giessen</a> near Wetzlar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>. The Minox B, with a built-in Gossen meter, became the most widely produced model, and in 1969 the Minox C was the first camera of its kind with electronic exposure control. A Minox B in a protective housing was used in 1961 to inspect the inside of the US Army's SL-1 reactor after its explosion. The last TLX models were available until September 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minox” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
